cmrgo4uu3004n5vox3zwwi6tk	cmrgo4utl004c5voxcr8nys4m	External key management for Azure Managed HSM is now in public preview	External key management for Azure Managed HSM is now in public preview\nAzure Key Vault Managed Hardware Security Module (HSM) provides strong sovereignty over your encryption keys. Keys are generated and stored in a single-tenant, FIPS 140-3 Level 3 HSM that only you control: Microsoft has no access to your key material, and you govern who can use each key. For most organizations, including those with stringent regulatory requirements, this level of control is sufficient. External key management for Azure Key Vault Managed HSM is now in public preview to address that requirement, delivering on a commitment made a year ago. Try External key management for Azure Key Vault Managed HSM How Managed HSM delivers sovereignty today Managed HSM already provides. Managed HSM is a single-tenant service: each instance is a dedicated cluster of FIPS 140-3 Level 3 validate	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:49.371	2026-07-11 18:01:29.838	Cloud	announcement	{"Azure Key Vault","Managed HSM",Microsoft,"FIPS 140-3",RSA,TLS}	launch external key management	{"Azure Key Vault","Managed HSM"}	Key Management	Azure Key Vault Managed Hardware Security Module (HSM) has introduced external key management, now in public preview. This feature allows organizations to maintain strict control over their encryption keys, generated and stored within FIPS 140-3 Level 3 HSMs, while ensuring that Microsoft has no access to the key material. The integration remains seamless for applications, giving users the freedom to choose their hardware and implementation while maintaining security with mutual TLS. This enhancement is crucial for organizations with strict regulatory requirements and reinforces their sovereignty over key management. Explore this open model to take full control of your encryption keys.
cmrgo4lav001d5voxix705qza	cmrgo4l9e000n5voxvy1lneq8	The White House's post-quantum executive order is an important milestone. It’s time to get to work	The White House's post-quantum executive order is an important milestone. It’s time to get to work\nOn June 22, 2026, President Trump signed Executive Order 14412, "Securing the Nation Against Advanced Cryptographic Attacks." The order sets a December 31, 2030, deadline for federal agencies to transition their most sensitive systems to post-quantum encryption, and a December 31, 2031, deadline for post-quantum authentication. The EO also directs federal contractors to comply with post-quantum Federal Information Processing Standards (FIPS) by the end of 2030. We welcome this executive order. The U.S. government has a long track record of using federal leadership and procurement to drive adoption of new technologies across the broader industry. We've seen this work with IPv6, with routing security and the Resource Public Key Infrastructure (RPKI), and with DNSSEC, and we’re glad to see th	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:37.015	2026-07-11 17:59:12.29	Security	announcement	{"Trump Administration","National Institute of Standards and Technology",Cloudflare,"Federal Information Processing Standards","Internet Engineering Task Force",IETF,"Quantum Key Distribution"}	sign executive order	{"Cloudflare One"}	Cryptography	On June 22, 2026, President Trump signed Executive Order 14412, which mandates federal agencies to transition to post-quantum encryption and authentication by specific deadlines (2030 for encryption, 2031 for authentication). This EO aims to mitigate risks associated with quantum computers capable of breaking current public-key cryptography. Companies including Cloudflare are already progressing towards this goal, emphasizing the importance of adopting NIST standards for a secure infrastructure. The initiative reflects a proactive government approach to safeguarding sensitive data against future quantum threats, urging organizations to start their migrations promptly.
