cmrgo4mrj003l5voxhcjzu302	cmrgo4mr300365voxlutvmlr8	Better tools made Copilot code review worse. Here’s how we actually improved it.	Better tools made Copilot code review worse. Here’s how we actually improved it.\nGive an agent better tools and it should do better work. That’s the instinct, anyway. When you open a pull request, Copilot code review reads the diff and explores the surrounding code to find the problems that matter before they ship. To do that, it used its own code exploration tools. So when we swapped in the better-maintained, shared tools that power the Copilot CLI, grep, glob, and view, we expected a clean upgrade. Instead, in our benchmarks, we found that the cost of reviews was higher and fewer issues were being caught. But the tools weren’t the problem. The instructions were. Once we rewrote them for the way a reviewer actually reads a pull request, the regression flipped into a win: roughly 20% lower average review cost, while maintaining the same review quality. This is the stor	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:38.911	2026-07-11 18:00:54.271	Developer Tools	update	{Copilot,"GitHub Copilot","GitHub Copilot cloud agent"}	rewriting tool instructions	{"Copilot code review","Copilot CLI"}	Code Review	The GitHub team improved the Copilot code review process by adjusting the tool instructions used during code evaluations. Initially, replacing the existing tools with shared Copilot CLI tools resulted in increased review costs and fewer detected issues. After identifying that the instructions were misaligned with reviewer workflows, they tailored the guidance to start from the diff, ask specific questions, and utilize the new tools effectively. This led to a 20% reduction in average review costs without compromising quality, demonstrating that effective tool implementation requires aligning guidance with specific tasks.
