cmrgo4lal000z5voxcvb55g45	cmrgo4l97000f5voxx5ysqoy7	Your Worker can now have its own cache in front of it	Your Worker can now have its own cache in front of it\nToday we are launching Workers Cache: a tiered cache that sits in front of your Worker, configured by a single line of Wrangler config and the same Cache-Control headers you already know. When Workers Cache is enabled, every cacheable request to your Worker hits Cloudflare's cache first. If there's a fresh cached response, Cloudflare returns it directly — your Worker doesn't run, and you don't pay CPU time for it. On a miss, your Worker runs, and if your response is cacheable, Cloudflare stores it for the next request. The next request from anywhere on Earth can be served straight from cache. The whole thing is one config block: { "name": "my-worker", "main": "src/index.ts", "compatibility_date": "2026-05-01", "cache": { "enabled": true } } After that, you control caching the way HTTP has 	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:37.005	2026-07-11 17:58:49.077	Cloud	announcement	{Cloudflare,Workers,Wrangler,Astro,Next.js,Remix,SvelteKit,"TanStack Start",HTTP,"Durable Objects"}	launch Workers Cache	{"Workers Cache",Wrangler}	Caching Architecture	Cloudflare has launched Workers Cache, a new tiered caching solution designed for server-rendered applications. This innovation allows cached responses to be served directly without invoking the Worker for hits, reducing CPU usage and latency. With easy configuration via Wrangler and support for standard HTTP headers, Workers Cache accelerates content delivery while maintaining dynamic capabilities. For server-rendered apps, it offers a solution to cache rendered responses on demand, blending the speed of static sites with the freshness of server renders. Cloudflare aims to enhance developer productivity and app performance through this effective caching layer.
