cmrgo4lan00135vox1jo37b76	cmrgo4l96000d5voxrq3j1ppi	Introducing Meerkat: an experiment in global consensus	Introducing Meerkat: an experiment in global consensus\nMany internal services at Cloudflare need to read and modify the same control-plane state from across our 330+ global data centers. They need guarantees that different readers never see inconsistent state, and that the system remains available for writes even when some data centers or links fail. But Cloudflare’s network runs across the entire Internet, and the Internet is an unpredictable place. Servers and data centers go down. Queues fill up. Links and cables get cut. These conditions make it difficult to run a globally available data system that guarantees strong consistency (e.g., that all readers are guaranteed to read all prior writes) because hostile conditions hinder distributed system replicas’ ability to reliably synchronize data with one another. One way to synchronize data saf	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:37.007	2026-07-11 17:58:55.205	Cloud	announcement	{Cloudflare,Meerkat,QuePaxa,Raft,Rust}	introducing Meerkat	{}	Distributed Systems	Cloudflare's Research team has introduced Meerkat, a new distributed consensus service utilizing the QuePaxa algorithm, designed to enhance the management of control-plane data across its global data centers. Meerkat allows all replicas to write concurrently, eliminating the single point of failure found in traditional consensus algorithms like Raft. This enables better availability even during network issues, essential for maintaining strong consistency in Cloudflare's systems. The upcoming year will see more detailed explorations of Meerkat's architecture and performance in subsequent blog posts.
