cmrgo4laq00155voxmcimaz1k	cmrgo4l9f000o5vox4d5sbb24	How we found a bug in the hyper HTTP library	How we found a bug in the hyper HTTP library\nThe Images service, built in Rust on Workers, runs on every machine in Cloudflare’s edge network. To handle client connections, we use hyper, an open-source HTTP library for Rust. Last year, we introduced the Images binding to enable custom, programmatic workflows for processing remote images in Workers. At the end of 2025, we rearchitected the binding to provide a more direct, local connection between the Workers runtime and the Images service. Shortly after rollout, we received reports that transformation requests from the binding were failing — but only intermittently and only for larger images. Even stranger, the responses for these requests returned a 200 status without any errors logged. The image data was simply cut short: A response that should have been two megabytes might arrive 	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:37.01	2026-07-11 17:58:58.892	Cloud	update	{Cloudflare,Rust,hyper,R2,"Images binding"}	fixing a race condition	{"Cloudflare Workers","Images API"}	Image Processing	Cloudflare recently addressed a race condition in its Images binding that caused intermittent failures for larger image transformation requests. The issue stemmed from the hyper library's connection lifecycle management, which led to truncated image data being sent back despite a 200 OK status. The fix involved modifying the connection shutdown process to ensure all data was flushed before closing the connection. This patch stabilizes the Images binding’s architecture and enhances its reliability, paving the way for future enhancements. Now developers can build more robust media-rich applications on Cloudflare.
