cmrgo4mkq002w5voxmpl92119	cmrgo4mjv002q5voxu3r3u7md	What is an SBOM (and Why Can’t You Ship Without One)?	What is an SBOM (and Why Can’t You Ship Without One)?\nIn Omdia’s 2026 software supply chain security report, 73% of organizations that generate SBOMs say they enable more efficient vulnerability mitigation, yet 86% still find the generation process challenging. That gap between recognized value and operational difficulty is where most teams are stuck. For teams building and securing containerized applications, understanding what an SBOM is, and how to make it useful, is no longer optional. This guide covers what SBOMs contain, why they matter for software supply chain security, how standard formats and tooling work, and where the industry is headed with regulations and enforcement. An SBOM is a machine-readable inventory of every component inside a software artifact. SBOMs gain real value when paired with provenance attestations and cryptogra	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:38.666	2026-07-11 18:00:17.153	Security	guide	{Omdia,"Executive Order 14028",CISA,"EU Cyber Resilience Act","OpenSSL Project",OWASP,SPDX,CycloneDX,BuildKit,Docker,Sonatype,JWP}	released security report	{"Docker Hardened Images","Docker Scout"}	Software Supply Chain	Omdia's 2026 software supply chain security report highlights the effectiveness of Software Bills of Materials (SBOMs) in vulnerability mitigation. While 73% of organizations producing SBOMs see their value, 86% find the generation process challenging. The report emphasizes that SBOMs, which provide a detailed inventory of software components, are crucial for incident response and regulatory compliance. Generating SBOMs at build time enhances visibility and simplifies vulnerability management. As regulations tighten, adopting SBOM processes is becoming essential for organizations involved in software development and deployment.
cmrgo4mkr002y5voxd5ei13ii	cmrgo4mju002o5vox6vslirvl	How to Generate an SBOM for Container Workflows	How to Generate an SBOM for Container Workflows\nAccording to Omdia’s 2026 software supply chain security report, 86% of organizations find SBOM generation challenging. A major driver is tool sprawl: teams cobbling together different scanners for different artifact types, getting inconsistent output across pipelines, and spending engineering time reconciling the results rather than acting on them. SBOMs have become important to how security teams respond to vulnerability disclosures, how compliance teams satisfy auditors, and how procurement decisions get made. That makes the generation step load-bearing. If the SBOM your pipeline produces misses transitive dependencies, records declared versions instead of resolved ones, or is not cryptographically bound to the artifact it describes, every downstream decision built on that data inherits	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:38.667	2026-07-11 18:00:21.435	Security	analysis	{Omdia,SPDX,CycloneDX,Docker,BuildKit}	release SBOM guidelines	{"Docker Hardened Images","Docker Scout"}	Software Supply Chain	Omdia’s 2026 report highlights that 86% of organizations face challenges in generating Software Bill of Materials (SBOM). The primary issue is tool sprawl, where multiple scanners yield inconsistent outputs. SBOMs are now critical for security responses, compliance, and procurement decisions, making their quality crucial. The report emphasizes the advantages of build-time SBOM generation, which captures resolved dependency states and avoids gaps often missed by post-build scanning. It also outlines that SBOM generation should integrate into CI/CD pipelines for automation and reliability, advising best practices to ensure actionable and reliable outputs. As regulations around SBOMs increase, adopting effective generation practices is becoming essential.
cmrgo4mkt00315voxrl7h4epx	cmrgo4mjw002u5voxpvu5285m	5 Software Supply Chain Security Best Practices for Development Teams	5 Software Supply Chain Security Best Practices for Development Teams\nUnderstanding software supply chain security is one thing. Putting it into practice across a real pipeline, with real deadlines and real constraints, is another. Most organizations recognize that their software supply chain is a growing attack surface, but translating that awareness into concrete, repeatable practices is where the work gets difficult. But why should your team tackle this now? According to Sonatype, over 99% of open source malware identified in 2025 occurred on npm. And the first self-replicating npm worm emerged, spreading autonomously across developer environments and compromising hundreds of packages within days. Meanwhile, Verizon’s 2025 Data Breach Investigations Report found that the share of breaches involving third parties doubled year-over-year to 30%. This guide f	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:38.669	2026-07-11 18:00:25.199	Security	guide	{Sonatype,npm,Verizon,"Docker Hardened Images",NIST,CISA,SLSA,OpenVEX}	launch supply chain security guide	{"Docker Hardened Images","Docker Scout"}	Supply Chain Security	A new guide emphasizes the importance of software supply chain security amidst increasing threats, particularly from open source malware targeting npm, which is expected to reach 99% by 2025. Organizations are urged to adopt best practices focusing on trusted content, build security, pre-deployment verification, and continuous monitoring to mitigate risks. Key recommendations include using minimally sized verified base images, continuous generation and integration of Software Bill of Materials (SBOMs), and establishing strict access control policies. The guide aims to help teams navigate the complexities of securing their software supply chains effectively.
cmry6b41n000f4wrtg1ozfqcx	cmry6b41l000e4wrtp1tdz6ol	The case for a cooldown: Why Dependabot now waits before issuing version updates	The case for a cooldown: Why Dependabot now waits before issuing version updates\nIn September 2025, an attacker phished the credentials of a single npm maintainer and published booby-trapped versions of chalk, debug, and around a dozen other packages that are together downloaded more than 2 billion times a week. The code rewrote cryptocurrency wallet addresses inside any browser app that loaded it. The poisoned versions were live for roughly two hours before the community caught them and npm pulled them. Two hours is a fast response. However, it is also more than enough time for an automated update tool to see the new version, open a pull request, and put it in front of your team, because version update tooling is built to grab the newest release the moment it lands. That pattern sits behind a growing share of supply chain attacks. The malicious code rides in on a bran	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-23 23:59:39.323	2026-07-23 23:59:45.299	Security	update	{Dependabot,npm,GitHub,"Solana web3.js",Axios,ua-parser-js,"Ledger Connect Kit"}	implement cooldown feature	{}	Supply Chain Security	In response to security risks associated with version updates, GitHub's Dependabot now introduces a three-day cooldown period before opening pull requests for non-security version updates. This change aims to reduce the likelihood of automatically integrating malicious releases into projects, particularly following recent supply chain attacks that exploit rapid deployment of compromised packages on npm. By delaying version updates, maintainers gain time to spot potential vulnerabilities, thereby enhancing security without overly delaying essential updates. The cooldown is adjustable, allowing flexibility in managing dependencies based on their trust level.
cmrgo4mkt00325voxpiablcqi	cmrgo4mjv002r5voxobz5tp7k	Docker Content Trust: Retirement and Migration Guidance	Docker Content Trust: Retirement and Migration Guidance\nTLDR: Docker Content Trust (DCT) and the Notary v1 service at notary.docker.io are being fully retired (first announced in July of 2025). This blog explains what is changing, who is affected, and how to move to modern alternatives. Ten years ago, Docker Content Trust (DCT) gave the container ecosystem one of its first ways to verify the integrity and publisher of an image, built on The Update Framework and the Notary v1 project. The upstream Notary v1 codebase is no longer maintained, more modern signing tools have become the standard, and today a very small number (fewer than 0.05%) of Docker Hub pulls rely on DCT. Last year we began retiring DCT for Docker Official Images, and now we’re completing that work by fully retiring DCT and the Notary v1 service at notary.docker.io. This post co	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:38.669	2026-07-11 18:00:29.883	Security	announcement	{"Docker Content Trust","Notary v1","The Update Framework",Sigstore,Cosign,"Notary Project",Harbor,Kyverno,Ratify,Gatekeeper}	retiring Docker Content Trust	{"Docker Hub","Docker Hardened Images"}	Container Signing	Docker has announced the retirement of Docker Content Trust (DCT) and the Notary v1 service, originally scheduled for July 2025. This decision stems from the lack of maintenance for the Notary v1 project and the recent shift toward modern signing tools like Sigstore/Cosign and the Notation Project. While most users will not be affected, those who configured DCT will need to migrate to modern alternatives before the complete shutdown. Recommendations include disabling DCT, using image digests for reproducibility, or adopting tools like Cosign and Notation for signing images. Docker aims to invest in more secure defaults and robust signing mechanisms for the future.
cmrgo4mri003h5voxmv8kediz	cmrgo4mr6003c5voxzm7z4dew	How GitHub used secret scanning to reach inbox zero	How GitHub used secret scanning to reach inbox zero\nSeveral years ago, GitHub Security launched an initiative to assess and improve our overall secrets hygiene. As part of that effort, we piloted the Secret Scanning capability that was under development at the time. That’s when we found more than 20,000 secrets spread across our 15,000+ repositories. The number was significantly higher than we anticipated, but it quickly became clear that success would depend on identifying which alerts represented real risk, assigning ownership, and remediating them safely. Nine months later, we reached zero open alerts. New secret scanning customers often ask us: “How do you manage this internally? How did you actually clean up your existing secrets?” Like many long-running software companies, GitHub’s approach to secrets management evolved over time. Git	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-11 17:58:38.91	2026-07-11 18:00:36.438	Security	case-study	{GitHub,"GitHub Advanced Security","GitHub tokens"}	clean up existing secrets	{"Secret Scanning","push protection"}	Secrets Management	GitHub's Security team undertook a comprehensive initiative to improve secrets hygiene, eliminating over 20,000 alerts across their repositories within nine months. By enabling secret scanning enterprise-wide and categorizing alerts, they effectively prioritized and triaged risks, focusing on genuine threats. Key phases included validating active credentials, establishing ownership for remediation, and automating processes for accountability. This approach emphasizes enduring ownership and collaborative responsibility, ensuring security practices evolve with organizational growth. GitHub's experience offers valuable strategies for other organizations tackling secrets management.
