cmrz7prgx02hv4wrt3n06f6rl	cmrz7prgr02hu4wrt92qkjh1i	BGP ORIGIN attribute manipulation and its impact on the Internet	BGP ORIGIN attribute manipulation and its impact on the Internet\nBorder Gateway Protocol (BGP) is the de facto routing protocol of the Internet. It offers built-in mechanisms to allow entities, represented by Autonomous Systems (ASes), to express how they want to send and receive traffic on the Internet. One such mechanism is path attributes, which carry essential routing information and metadata for their associated route. The path selection algorithm processes some of these path attributes in a deterministic sequence to calculate the best path for this specific prefix. Using our unique position on the Internet, we took an investigative look at one of the well-known mandatory attributes in BGP, the ORIGIN attribute. ORIGIN must be present in every BGP prefix announcement and should not be modified by any router after being set by the originating one. W	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-24 17:26:48.657	2026-07-24 17:26:53.268	Networking	analysis	{"Border Gateway Protocol","Autonomous Systems","RIPE RIS",RouteViews,RFC4271,"Tier-1 ASes",CAIDA}	conducted ORIGIN attribute experiments	{BGPKIT,"Multi-threaded Routing Toolkit"}	BGP	The article investigates the manipulation of the ORIGIN attribute in the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP), revealing that about 70% of observed paths have a different ORIGIN value than initially set. This manipulation alters traffic routing significantly, as networks change ORIGIN values to gain competitive advantages. Despite the guidance against modifying ORIGIN by RFC4271, many Autonomous Systems (ASes) do so for profit. The findings raise questions about the relevance of the ORIGIN attribute in today's Internet, suggesting a need for its potential deprecation or reevaluation in BGP path selection processes.
cmrjjmcrk070z5voxwvqtptpx	cmrjjmcre070y5voxuc9z0egz	Amazon SQS turns 20: Two decades of reliable messaging at scale	Amazon SQS turns 20: Two decades of reliable messaging at scale\nOn July 13, 2006, we launched Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) as one of the first three services available to customers, alongside Amazon EC2 and Amazon S3. We had learned firsthand that distributed systems need a reliable way to pass messages between components without creating tight dependencies. If one service called another directly and that service was slow or unavailable, failures cascaded through the entire system. Message queuing solved this by letting services communicate asynchronously: a producer could drop a message into a queue and move on, while a consumer picked it up when ready. This approach kept individual service failures from affecting the rest of the system. When Amazon SQS launched publicly in July 2006, it made this pattern available to every AWS customer. T	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-13 18:15:46.208	2026-07-13 18:15:51.955	Cloud	announcement	{"Amazon SQS","Amazon EC2","Amazon S3","AWS SDK","AWS Lambda","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon Bedrock"}	Launch new features	{"Amazon SQS","Amazon S3"}	Message Queuing	Amazon SQS celebrated its 20th anniversary with a series of feature enhancements that improve its messaging service functionality. Key updates include increased transaction capabilities, expanded encryption options, and support for larger message payloads. The introduction of fair queues addresses multi-tenant disruptions, while the Extended Client Library for Python brings expanded support for processing larger payloads. These advancements maintain SQS's foundational role in decoupling services and enhancing resilience in modern workflows, including those involving AI tasks. Amazon SQS continues to evolve, adapting to customer needs over the years.
