cms4ue5i00hee4wrtvj9fhytj	cms4ue5hy0hec4wrtsohlke35	Disrupting supply chain attacks on npm and GitHub Actions	Disrupting supply chain attacks on npm and GitHub Actions\nIn the past year, there’s been a pattern of supply chain attacks that target weaknesses in package repositories and CI/CD systems to quickly spread malware to hundreds of open source projects. This malware seeks to exfiltrate credentials both to broadly spread the attack, as well as for later exploitation. We’ve written a few times about our plans for hardening the supply chain: Our plan for a more secure npm supply chain in September 2025, Strengthening supply chain security: Preparing for the next malware campaign in December 2025, and What’s coming to our GitHub Actions 2026 security roadmap in March 2026. In this post, we’re updating you on changes we’ve implemented that directly disrupt some of the most common and impactful supply chain attack techniques. Anatomy of supply chain attac	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-28 16:00:29.016	2026-07-28 16:00:33.515	Security	update	{npm,"GitHub Actions",CircleCI,Dependabot}	Update security measures	{npm,"GitHub Actions"}	Supply Chain Security	GitHub is updating its security measures to disrupt supply chain attacks targeting open source projects via npm and GitHub Actions. These attacks exploit vulnerabilities in package repositories and CI/CD systems to spread malware and exfiltrate credentials. New measures include preventive account protections, safer defaults for GitHub Actions workflows, and enhancements to npm's publishing process. These changes aim to limit the impact of such attacks and help maintainers respond more effectively. GitHub emphasizes its commitment to improving open source security for developers and organizations.
cmscf3uq710pw4wrtazwtmw6d	cmscf3uq210pv4wrt2uoxaqfi	Welcome to Agents Week	Welcome to Agents Week\nThis week is Agents Week. As we started thinking about and planning the week, we wrestled with a broader question of what it means to support this new era of agents and what a purpose-built foundation for agents actually looks like. Which brought us to a simpler framing: what is an Agent Cloud? We quickly realized however, that our framing was wrong. Not because it’s the wrong question to ask, but because of who we were asking — ourselves, instead of our agents. It’s no longer about us and what we think, but about what agents need. That, in a nutshell, is what Agents Week is about. The cloud we have today, and the web it sits on, were built for people. Every layer assumes a human is watching: pages designed to hold your attention, dashboards to click through, interfaces tuned for how we re	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-08-02 23:14:43.663	2026-08-02 23:14:51.149	Cloud	other	{"Agent Cloud",agents,adlc,sdlc}	explore Agent Cloud	{}	Agent Cloud	This week is focused on Agents Week, where the main theme is defining an Agent Cloud that addresses the distinct needs of agents rather than humans. The week aims to explore how to structure this cloud for both agents and humans, ensuring it facilitates an agent-native future while accommodating the current human-centered web. Organizations are encouraged to ask their agents what they require from an Agent Cloud, fostering a dialogue about its features and capabilities. Stay tuned for more insights on innovations around Agents throughout the week.
cms94seic0tkw4wrt5lho9z17	cms94sei70tku4wrt7fmw9ibt	Don’t stop early: Case-folding source code at memory speed	Don’t stop early: Case-folding source code at memory speed\nSuppose a user searches for café and your corpus contains CAFÉ, or they type straße and you’ve stored STRASSE. To make these count as matches, you need a canonical form that erases case distinctions, so that two strings which differ only in case compare equal. That form is case folding, and it shows up wherever text is matched rather than displayed: search engines, regex (?i) flags, case-insensitive usernames and hostnames. It’s a basic operation, but at GitHub we run it a lot. Blackbird, GitHub’s code search engine, indexes over 180 million repositories—more than 480TB of source code. Every byte is case-folded before we extract ngrams and build the index, and for every potential query result, another (implicit or explicit) case folding operation is needed to locate matches. At that scale,	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-31 16:02:34.74	2026-07-31 16:02:39.912	Developer Tools	analysis	{GitHub,Blackbird,Rust,Unicode,ICU,CJK,U+023A,U+023E}	released Rust crate	{casefold}	Text Processing	GitHub has released an open-source Rust crate named 'casefold' that optimizes case folding operations crucial for text comparisons in its code search engine, Blackbird. This crate aims to enhance performance by eliminating unnecessary early exits in the ASCII handling process and utilizing byte-space arithmetic instead of traditional character decoding. The result achieves memory bandwidth speeds of over 45 GiB/s for common ASCII text, significantly outperforming existing solutions. This efficient approach is particularly beneficial for handling large volumes of source code within GitHub's extensive repository index.
cms95wko00to84wrtqusv86iq	cms95wknv0to64wrtcf48f4d0	Docker OIDC connections for GitHub Actions available for Docker Orgs	Docker OIDC connections for GitHub Actions available for Docker Orgs\nEliminate Stored Credentials in Your CI/CD Pipelines TL;DR: Docker now supports OpenID Connect (OIDC) for GitHub Actions. Your workflows can authenticate with short-lived, per-run tokens instead of stored PATs or OATs. No secrets to rotate, no credentials to leak. GitHub OIDC connections are available to organizations with Docker Team, Docker Business, or Docker Hardened Images (DHI) subscriptions, as well as organizations enrolled in the Docker Sponsored Open Source Program (DSOS). Table of contents The problem with stored credentials Who should use this How OIDC connections work Getting started What doesn’t change Learn more OIDC token exchange flow between GitHub Actions and Docker The problem with stored credentials Every GitHub Actions workflow that pushes or pulls images from Docker 	\N	\N	EXTRACTED	2026-07-31 16:33:48.96	2026-07-31 16:33:52.786	DevOps	release	{Docker,GitHub,AWS,GCP}	support OpenID Connect authentication	{docker/login-action}	CI/CD	Docker has introduced support for OpenID Connect (OIDC) authentication in GitHub Actions, enabling workflows to use short-lived tokens instead of long-lived personal access tokens (PATs) or organization access tokens (OATs). This change enhances security by eliminating the need for stored credentials, reducing the risk of leaks and the burden of manual token rotation. Organizations with certain Docker subscriptions can leverage these features through a straightforward setup in Docker Home. This move aligns Docker's practices with those already adopted by AWS and GCP for similar use cases, providing a more secure and efficient authentication method for container registry access.
